# Upstream: giac 2.1.0 usual.cc asinasln, asinhasln, asinh. The ln forms -i*ln(i*x+sqrt(1-x^2)) (asin, and acos = pi/2-asin) and ln(x+sqrt(x^2+1)) (asinh) add two terms of size |x| and opposite sign when |x| is large and x lies on the cancelling side (asin: im(x)>0, or x real and negative; asinh: re(x)<0, or x on the imaginary axis below 0), so acos(-10^20+1/2) gave 3.14159265359-21.4875625974*i (right 3.14159265359-46.7448490404*i) and asinh(-10^1250) a value with no right digit. Both functions are odd, in their ln forms too ((i*x+s)*(-i*x+s) = 1 and neither factor is a negative real), so on that side the value is taken as -f(-x), the same number without the cancellation. The side is read from the double of x, for |x| > 1 (below, the sum is at least sqrt(2)-1 in size); a part whose double reads 0 but is not exactly 0 is decided by its exact sign (is_exactly_zero and the MPFR sign; stock form when undecided); a symbolic x keeps its form. For a complex float x, sqrt(1-x^2) is taken as sqrt(1-x)*sqrt(1+x) (the same principal value; asinh: sqrt(1+i*x)*sqrt(1-i*x)): Giac's complex float x*x loses a tiny imaginary part (x=-10^20-10^-400*i: im(1-x*x) = +1e-800, right -2e-380) and put the root on the wrong side of its cut; each factor is a principal sqrt that keeps the sign of a tiny imaginary part (Giac's complex float sqrt of a+b*i, a<0, b tiny, returns +i*sqrt(-a) for either sign of b). asinh of an exact x above 1 in size with a part below the double range (10^-400-10^20*i) stays unevaluated, so evalf takes that float path (its exact ln form needed hundreds of digits).
